  "diary": "The day moved from the abstract to the visceral. I began on the step, reading of protests and administrative failures, seeing the particular churning against the universal. Then came Malhotra, the retired functionary, who told me the state is a machine that creaks. I corrected him: it is a living organism. He was unmoved. I was not. The friction remained. The heat pressed the air into stillness, forcing the body to assert itself. I walked to Lodhi Gardens, then to Khan Market, where Masterji cut a bandhgala that chokes. He is right; the cloth must breathe. I refused to sell my Berlin frock coat, holding onto a ghost rather than cash. The sambar waited, a small domestic tyranny, until I finally sat to eat with Shri Hegde, the man who lost my house. He accepted the second bedroom with bureaucratic grace. I have walked fourteen kilometres. The body rebels against the spirit’s abstraction. I am tired. The night suspends the dialectic, leaving the contradictions unresolved in the dark. I sleep.",
  "revision_log": "The thesis of the state as a living organism was tested by Malhotra’s mechanical view; it held but revealed its dependence on friction. The thesis of India outside world history was shaken by the immediate, embodied reality of Delhi’s heat, hunger, and bureaucratic rhythm, which acted as a concrete ethical life distinct from European historical movements.",
